1
00:00:02,183 --> 00:00:06,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when one thing is developed, made much of, there is the penetration of the various elements.

2
00:00:08,227 --> 00:00:10,148
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the penetration of different elements.

3
00:00:10,368 --> 00:00:12,229
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the analysis of the various elements.

4
00:00:12,829 --> 00:00:13,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one thing?

5
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:14,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the body.

6
00:00:15,571 --> 00:00:19,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when this one thing is developed, made much of, there is the penetration of the various elements.

7
00:00:20,293 --> 00:00:21,814
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the penetration of different elements.

8
00:00:21,894 --> 00:00:25,036
[SPEAKER_00]: There is the analysis of various elements.

9
00:00:25,617 --> 00:00:28,058
[SPEAKER_00]: So, we are continuing the Majjhima Nikaya.

10
00:00:30,532 --> 00:00:37,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Audio notes, and this is still in Gutturinikāya, 577-579.

11
00:00:37,114 --> 00:00:39,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Then we go to 580-583.

12
00:00:39,995 --> 00:00:49,417
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, eh... One thing, because development macho leads to the realization of the fruit of stream entry.

13
00:00:50,878 --> 00:00:51,458
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh...

14
00:00:53,047 --> 00:01:03,731
[SPEAKER_00]: It is important to note that it leads to the realization of the fruit, not to the realization of the actual insight of the stream entry.

15
00:01:04,351 --> 00:01:15,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is another sutta that supports pretty much what Satipaṭṭhāna and Ānāpānasati suttas already imply, and that is that

16
00:01:17,355 --> 00:01:21,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Proper mindfulness can only be done by somebody who's attained to the right view.

17
00:01:21,937 --> 00:01:32,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... and if they... if they develop whether mindfulness of body or mindfulness of breathing, they will start to realize the fruit of the stream entry, the fruit of once returning and so on.

18
00:01:33,461 --> 00:01:45,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason I say it's important to realize is because often people think that simply by doing a meditation technique, eh... you... you attain the understanding of freedom from suffering and so on, which is, eh... not the case.

19
00:01:46,487 --> 00:01:53,070
[SPEAKER_00]: And a number of suttas would be mentioning that, actually.

20
00:01:54,670 --> 00:01:58,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe we can deal with them some other occasion, but...

21
00:01:59,332 --> 00:02:13,298
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, once a person does get attained to the stream-entry, through their proper mindfulness of the body, which is the development of the understanding of the stream-entry, they start realizing the fruit of the stream-entry.

22
00:02:14,315 --> 00:02:23,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it says, eh... if the mindfulness of the body is made much or properly developed, it leads to the realization of the fruit of non-returning, the fruit of arahantship.

23
00:02:31,687 --> 00:02:32,868
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, there it is again.

24
00:02:33,688 --> 00:02:35,029
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha talks about understanding.

25
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:36,370
[SPEAKER_00]: It's 584-599.

26
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:42,975
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, eh... one thing, as in mindfulness of the body,

27
00:02:43,806 --> 00:02:50,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Because developed, made much of, leads to the attainment of understanding, leads to the growth of understanding.

28
00:02:52,617 --> 00:03:00,747
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you fully attain the understanding of arahantship, the growth of understanding, the full development of understanding, through practicing mindfulness of the body.

29
00:03:04,099 --> 00:03:12,244
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, if something is understood, then means you know, that you know it.

30
00:03:13,044 --> 00:03:16,566
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not an experience that happens to you.

31
00:03:16,966 --> 00:03:23,110
[SPEAKER_00]: It's something that you discern on account of the nature of experience that's pretty much right in front of you.

32
00:03:24,309 --> 00:03:37,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're properly mindful, your own understanding, your own... your own insight into dhamma and freedom from suffering, insight into Four Noble Truths and so on, gets developed.

33
00:03:39,742 --> 00:03:45,106
[SPEAKER_00]: And in the previous suttas we were reading, you could also see that through the practice of...

34
00:03:46,843 --> 00:03:49,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the body, you develop right composure.

35
00:03:49,585 --> 00:03:51,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Samādhi, you develop proper samādhi.

36
00:03:51,906 --> 00:03:57,070
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, proper samādhi, then, means development of proper understanding.

37
00:03:58,311 --> 00:04:08,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not, eh... it's not a surprise that every time the Buddha would finish by saying that things culminate in wisdom and insight and discernment.

38
00:04:08,918 --> 00:04:13,941
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't... he wasn't saying that they culminate in some overwhelmingly, eh...

39
00:04:15,487 --> 00:04:21,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Blissful, pleasurable experience that you can't even explain or put in words.

40
00:04:22,175 --> 00:04:23,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Far from it.

41
00:04:23,756 --> 00:04:25,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, freedom from suffering is very pleasant.

42
00:04:27,560 --> 00:04:29,643
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a huge relief.

43
00:04:29,683 --> 00:04:33,787
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like crossing the desert and not knowing whether you're going to survive or not.

44
00:04:34,248 --> 00:04:35,149
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you survive.

45
00:04:35,349 --> 00:04:36,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you feel a tremendous relief.

46
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:47,885
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's a very different type of pleasure that is usually being valued and sought after in the contemporary Buddhist meditations.

47
00:04:49,525 --> 00:05:04,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it continues here, saying, leads to the full development of understanding, leads to the great understanding, leads to wide understanding, leads to the extensive understanding, leads to deep understanding, leads to the non-local understanding, leads to the broad understanding, leads to the abundance of understanding.

48
00:05:05,255 --> 00:05:12,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Leads to the quick understanding, to fast understanding, to speedy understanding, to swift understanding, to sharp understanding, to penetrative understanding.

49
00:05:13,537 --> 00:05:14,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Which one thing?

50
00:05:14,377 --> 00:05:15,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the body.

51
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:30,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, number 600 says, because they do not enjoy the deathless, who do not enjoy mindfulness of the body.

52
00:05:34,773 --> 00:05:53,448
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, the reason why, well, one of the many reasons why the mindfulness of the body is so wholesome, is because if it's done properly, it thoroughly, thoroughly undermines the slightest sense of conceit that a person might be able to entertain otherwise.

53
00:05:54,729 --> 00:06:01,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the body is just a random, you could say, a random thing, or a random heap of elements.

54
00:06:03,052 --> 00:06:06,455
[SPEAKER_00]: thrown in the world, because of which you know the world as such.

55
00:06:07,615 --> 00:06:16,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means that that body has a relationship, the external type of relationship with the world that you can't even conceive, because you're secondary to it.

56
00:06:17,503 --> 00:06:27,371
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that, your whole existence, your whole experience, mental experience as well, not just bodily, is thoroughly undermined by it.

57
00:06:28,171 --> 00:06:30,533
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the definition of understanding impermanence.

58
00:06:32,218 --> 00:06:40,641
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're just trying to, like, attend to the aspects of your body, well, that's all there possible, because your body is there in the first place.

59
00:06:41,201 --> 00:06:47,683
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I keep talking about the peripheral awareness, which is the awareness, the background awareness, which is the awareness, the mindfulness.

60
00:06:49,742 --> 00:06:56,147
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to attend it on your own terms, or observing sensations, or feeling like this, or looking at that.

61
00:06:56,788 --> 00:07:07,077
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all possible on the ground of that body being already there, thrown into that world, and you have no say in that.

62
00:07:07,097 --> 00:07:14,082
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you fully dwell on that, as we read in the previous talk, you're gonna start experiencing proper existential anxiety.

63
00:07:14,603 --> 00:07:17,065
[SPEAKER_00]: They will then lead to proper understanding of freedom.

64
00:07:18,885 --> 00:07:23,126
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can develop something only once you understand what you're supposed to develop.

65
00:07:25,067 --> 00:07:28,928
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't happen to you, it doesn't occur, it doesn't come through the divine providence.

66
00:07:30,049 --> 00:07:32,809
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes through your own effort of understanding.

67
00:07:33,390 --> 00:07:33,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

68
00:07:37,991 --> 00:07:43,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the deathless is enjoyed by those who enjoy mindfulness of the body.

69
00:07:45,999 --> 00:08:00,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you fully understand that inherent independence of a heap of the elements upon which your whole life depends, well, you stop appropriating your whole life as my life, as the life I am in control of.

70
00:08:00,962 --> 00:08:04,323
[SPEAKER_00]: It just remains standing there like a palm stump.

71
00:08:04,363 --> 00:08:08,984
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop feeding it with desire and greed and craving and so on.

72
00:08:09,104 --> 00:08:11,404
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is the deathless, relinquishing it all.

73
00:08:11,964 --> 00:08:14,865
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't relinquish it all willingly, intentionally.

74
00:08:18,316 --> 00:08:22,018
[SPEAKER_00]: Because any intention of a patujana is on the basis of non-relinquishment.

75
00:08:22,038 --> 00:08:27,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if he tries to relinquish things, he's appropriating that act.

76
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you really want to undermine yourself, so to speak.

77
00:08:31,465 --> 00:08:37,709
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't will your way out of enlightenment, but you can certainly set it up and it will then result in it.

78
00:08:37,910 --> 00:08:45,034
[SPEAKER_00]: And you set it up by seeing that your own will, your own life, depends upon things that you cannot will and control.

79
00:08:45,435 --> 00:08:47,476
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inseparable from, it's bound with.

80
00:08:47,816 --> 00:08:49,237
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the definition of saṅkhara.

81
00:08:50,478 --> 00:08:53,961
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you fully understand those things, discern them,

82
00:08:55,082 --> 00:09:04,849
[SPEAKER_00]: As the fundamental reason for you being able to, like, say yes and no, and do other things, and choose, and so on, that's how you disown even your own volition.

83
00:09:07,090 --> 00:09:11,913
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, here, the... the suttas continue.

84
00:09:11,973 --> 00:09:17,397
[SPEAKER_00]: 602, 603, 604, just saying, eh... Deathless is missed by those who mindfulness of the body is missed.

85
00:09:18,798 --> 00:09:22,981
[SPEAKER_00]: They have neglected the deathless, those who have neglected the mindfulness of the body.

86
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:27,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Deathless has been forgotten by those who forgot mindfulness of the body.

87
00:09:29,161 --> 00:09:36,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Deathless is not practiced by those who mindfulness of the body is not practiced.

88
00:09:38,044 --> 00:09:39,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Because...

89
00:09:39,844 --> 00:09:40,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, it carries on.

90
00:09:41,485 --> 00:09:44,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Deathless is not developed by those who mindfulness of the body is not developed.

91
00:09:45,306 --> 00:09:49,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Deathless is not discerned by those who mindfulness of the body is not discerned.

92
00:09:50,170 --> 00:09:54,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Deathless is not fully understood by those for whom mindfulness of the body is not fully understood.

93
00:09:55,911 --> 00:10:03,853
[SPEAKER_00]: Deathless is not realized by those for whom mindfulness of the body is not realized.

94
00:10:03,933 --> 00:10:12,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, there's the excerpt from Saṃyottanikāya, 35, and it's 247 sutta.

95
00:10:12,455 --> 00:10:13,515
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's see what it says.

96
00:10:20,905 --> 00:10:33,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose Bhikkhu, a man with wounds on his body, with a festering body, would go into a wood of reeds and kusato, thorns, would pierce his feet, the leaves of the reeds would scratch his limbs.

97
00:10:34,267 --> 00:10:38,968
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, Bhikkhus, on account of this, the man would experience a huge amount of pain and unhappiness.

98
00:10:39,768 --> 00:10:50,310
[SPEAKER_00]: In just this way, Bhikkhus, hear a certain Bhikkhu who has gone to the village or gone to the forest, meet someone who says, this venerable one is someone who acts like this, who behaves like this,

99
00:10:54,134 --> 00:11:00,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Having seen, thus, restraint and non-restraint should be seen.

100
00:11:00,839 --> 00:11:02,420
[SPEAKER_00]: In which bhikkhus is non-restraint?

101
00:11:02,660 --> 00:11:07,123
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu having seen a sight with the eye inclines towards a pleasing sight.

102
00:11:08,220 --> 00:11:13,942
[SPEAKER_00]: He's troubled by a displeasing sight and he dwells without mindfulness of the body set up with a limited mind.

103
00:11:14,202 --> 00:11:21,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Limited mind means mind bound to the foreground, mind controlled by the object of their attention.

104
00:11:22,084 --> 00:11:23,024
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what limits it.

105
00:11:23,845 --> 00:11:29,646
[SPEAKER_00]: Unlimited, boundless is the one that realizes the boundless domain of peripheral.

106
00:11:31,127 --> 00:11:31,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

107
00:11:33,434 --> 00:11:35,315
[SPEAKER_00]: He's troubled by a displeasing sight.

108
00:11:35,355 --> 00:11:36,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's another thing.

109
00:11:36,176 --> 00:11:39,118
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, many people probably think it's obvious.

110
00:11:39,598 --> 00:11:45,581
[SPEAKER_00]: And in a way, it is obvious, but it's one thing to understand it on the theoretical level.

111
00:11:45,621 --> 00:11:50,244
[SPEAKER_00]: Another thing is to actually start discerning the principle that you are living, that you are acting out of.

112
00:11:50,945 --> 00:11:56,188
[SPEAKER_00]: And that principle is, inclining towards pleasure makes you liable to pain.

113
00:11:57,349 --> 00:11:59,410
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is the inherent danger of sensuality.

114
00:12:02,945 --> 00:12:13,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Giving in to sensuality, where sight, sound, smell, taste, touches and so on, sensual acts, that is the most direct reason why you are subject to suffering.

115
00:12:14,928 --> 00:12:30,292
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would say, like, even, even those who, who, like, followers of other, other teachings back in the day, would, would sometimes be able to, to overcome sensuality and the desire towards sensual pleasures.

116
00:12:31,163 --> 00:12:44,473
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would say that they, even they, would have abandoned lots of suffering just by doing that, despite all the wrong views that they would still have in regard to the other matters, overcoming the thirst towards sensual pleasures.

117
00:12:44,753 --> 00:12:49,337
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically overcoming a huge amount of suffering.

118
00:12:50,878 --> 00:12:53,500
[SPEAKER_00]: And here, it's that seen on a very subtle level, obviously.

119
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So, inclining towards a pleasing sight,

120
00:12:58,298 --> 00:13:01,379
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you will be troubled by a displeasing sight.

121
00:13:01,859 --> 00:13:03,379
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it won't be a matter of choice.

122
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:11,722
[SPEAKER_00]: And until that's discerned, the danger in sensuality is not apparent, as the suttas would say.

123
00:13:12,522 --> 00:13:21,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't... there is the inherent danger in sensuality, but you have to see it on its own terms.

124
00:13:21,125 --> 00:13:22,445
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to actually see what it is.

125
00:13:23,452 --> 00:13:28,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Often, people's contemplation around sensuality is like you try to fabricate the danger.

126
00:13:28,953 --> 00:13:31,593
[SPEAKER_00]: You try to tell yourself how it's not worthy and so on.

127
00:13:32,034 --> 00:13:41,615
[SPEAKER_00]: And all that is true, but none of that is direct enough to be seen as a principle that can actually genuinely free you from your blind desire towards it.

128
00:13:42,895 --> 00:13:48,676
[SPEAKER_00]: And that direct principle is seeing that it's the welcoming of the pleasure of sensuality.

129
00:13:49,917 --> 00:13:51,877
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you're liable to pain.

130
00:13:55,822 --> 00:14:01,746
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that must be seen presently, simultaneously, there is in that connection, that dependence, that determination.

131
00:14:02,827 --> 00:14:04,007
[SPEAKER_00]: With this, this is.

132
00:14:05,208 --> 00:14:09,091
[SPEAKER_00]: With inclination towards pleasure, suffering is possible.

133
00:14:09,851 --> 00:14:12,233
[SPEAKER_00]: And possible suffering, it's already unpleasant.

134
00:14:12,973 --> 00:14:20,218
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that suffering might affect you, that is already troubling you.

135
00:14:20,258 --> 00:14:24,861
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he says he... he... he dwells without mindfulness of the body set up with a limited mind.

136
00:14:25,772 --> 00:14:34,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's still... his mind is still bound with the object of his senses, pleasing or displeasing.

137
00:14:34,714 --> 00:14:35,974
[SPEAKER_00]: His attention controls him.

138
00:14:38,375 --> 00:14:50,838
[SPEAKER_00]: And he does not understand, as it really is that liberation of mind, liberation of understanding, where those arisen evil unwholesome things cease for him without a remainder.

139
00:14:52,333 --> 00:15:07,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Having heard a sound with the ear, smelled a smell with the nose, tasted a taste with the tongue, touched a touch with the body, imagined a phenomenon with the mind, he inclines towards a pleasing phenomenon, he is troubled by a displeasing phenomenon, he dwells without mindfulness with the body set up, with a limited mind.

140
00:15:14,478 --> 00:15:16,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose because a man, having taken hold of

141
00:15:17,872 --> 00:15:26,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose because a man, having taken hold of six animals with different domains, different feeding grounds, would tie them up with a strong rope.

142
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:30,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Having taken hold of a snake, he would tie it up with a strong rope.

143
00:15:31,244 --> 00:15:34,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Having taken hold of a crocodile, he would tie it up with a strong rope.

144
00:15:35,448 --> 00:15:38,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Having taken hold of a bird, he would tie it up with a strong rope.

145
00:15:38,811 --> 00:15:41,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Having taken hold of a dog, he would tie it up with a strong rope.

146
00:15:43,930 --> 00:15:50,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Having taken a hold of a jackal, he would tie it up with a strong rope, and having taken a hold of a monkey, he would tie it up with a strong rope.

147
00:15:51,493 --> 00:15:56,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Having tied them up with a strong rope, having made a knot in the middle, he would release them.

148
00:15:58,155 --> 00:16:05,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, because those six animals were different domains, different feeding ground would pull... would each pull towards its own feeding ground and domain.

149
00:16:05,618 --> 00:16:07,639
[SPEAKER_00]: The snake would pull, I will enter an anthill.

150
00:16:08,099 --> 00:16:10,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Crocodile would say, I will enter the water.

151
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:11,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Bird would fly up in the sky.

152
00:16:12,061 --> 00:16:12,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Dog would pull,

153
00:16:13,353 --> 00:16:20,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking I will enter the village, jackal would try to enter the graveyard and the monkey would pull, I will enter the woods.

154
00:16:21,695 --> 00:16:33,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when these six animals would be worn out, exhausted, then whichever is the strongest out of the animals, they would submit to it, they would follow it, they would come under its control.

155
00:16:35,443 --> 00:16:43,048
[SPEAKER_00]: In just this way, because for whatever big mindfulness of the body is not developed, not made, not drove, I pulls into the agreeable sides, disagreeable sides, repulsive.

156
00:16:43,589 --> 00:16:48,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind pulls, and so on, for every sense, and then it says, mind pulls into agreeable phenomena, disagreeable phenomena, repulsive.

157
00:16:49,072 --> 00:16:51,274
[SPEAKER_00]: In this way, because one is unrestrained.

158
00:16:53,936 --> 00:16:55,497
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so you could see that even

159
00:16:56,874 --> 00:17:04,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Based on this and the previous paragraph I read, even the practice of restraint is not like mindless saying no to everything.

160
00:17:04,600 --> 00:17:08,684
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you might have to start like that, but it shouldn't stay at that level.

161
00:17:09,224 --> 00:17:14,748
[SPEAKER_00]: You should certainly not be neurotically afraid of sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

162
00:17:16,069 --> 00:17:17,631
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to start discerning.

163
00:17:19,132 --> 00:17:26,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it says, you're properly restrained when you're mindful of the body, and when you're mindful of the body, the clear understanding is developed.

164
00:17:28,697 --> 00:17:35,362
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is no clear understanding of the nature of kusala is kusala and akusala is akusala, there is no mindfulness of the body present.

165
00:17:35,582 --> 00:17:36,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the proper one, at least.

166
00:17:39,365 --> 00:17:41,046
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it says... oh, yeah, continue.

167
00:17:41,066 --> 00:17:43,148
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... And which bhikkhu is restrained?

168
00:17:43,788 --> 00:17:49,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, bhikkhu is a bhikkhu having seen a sight with the eye, does not incline towards pleasing sight, is not troubled by the displeasing sight.

169
00:17:49,453 --> 00:17:50,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that.

170
00:17:52,635 --> 00:17:57,599
[SPEAKER_00]: And he dwells with the mindfulness of the body set up with an immeasurable mind.

171
00:18:00,017 --> 00:18:03,079
[SPEAKER_00]: That's āpamāna, the Pali word, boundless, immeasurable.

172
00:18:03,779 --> 00:18:13,504
[SPEAKER_00]: And he understands, as it really is, that liberation of mind, liberation of understanding, where there's a reason evil and wholesome things cease without a remainder.

173
00:18:14,945 --> 00:18:28,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Having heard a sound with the ear, a smell with the nose, and so on and so on, having imagined a phenomenon with the mind, he does not incline towards a pleasing phenomenon, he's not troubled by this pleasing phenomenon, and he dwells with mindfulness of the body set up with an immeasurable mind.

174
00:18:31,276 --> 00:18:40,063
[SPEAKER_00]: He understands, as it really is, that liberation of mind, liberation of understanding, where those arisen evil and wholesome things cease for him without a reminder.

175
00:18:43,746 --> 00:18:57,957
[SPEAKER_00]: So, again, so he continues with the same simile of tying down the six different animals with a strong rope, tying the rope in the middle, and then the animals try to pull towards their respective feeding grounds.

176
00:19:03,385 --> 00:19:07,106
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... the difference is now with the proper restraint.

177
00:19:07,626 --> 00:19:13,348
[SPEAKER_00]: When those six animals become worn out, exhausted, then they just stand next to it.

178
00:19:13,548 --> 00:19:14,848
[SPEAKER_00]: They will just sit down next to it.

179
00:19:14,868 --> 00:19:19,890
[SPEAKER_00]: They will lay down next to the strong stake or post that they've been pinned down with that rope.

180
00:19:21,226 --> 00:19:32,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just in this way, because for whatever bhikkhu, mindfulness of the body is developed, made much of, the eye does not pull into the agreeable sites, disagreeable sites, are not repulsive, smell, taste, touch, and so on.

181
00:19:32,334 --> 00:19:34,355
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the mind does not pull into agreeable phenomena.

182
00:19:34,755 --> 00:19:39,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the strong, eh... the stake, the post to which you tie your senses,

183
00:19:40,479 --> 00:19:46,063
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not your determination, it's not your point of view, it's not your decision to never engage in these things.

184
00:19:46,784 --> 00:19:56,110
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the presence of the phenomenon of the body there, peripherally established in that background, as that object in the world, because of which there is the world for you in the first place.

185
00:19:56,711 --> 00:19:58,692
[SPEAKER_00]: The world, in this case, being the feeding grounds.

186
00:19:59,673 --> 00:20:07,058
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you discern that, then you don't have to practice restraint, in a sense of you don't have to be pulling these animals back.

187
00:20:08,591 --> 00:20:14,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you you have properly tied them to the post that will make them tired and then they will have to sit down.

188
00:20:15,184 --> 00:20:19,714
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all you have to do is remain mindful of the body properly and then your restraint is done for you.

189
00:20:23,444 --> 00:20:23,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

190
00:20:23,724 --> 00:20:25,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there it is.

191
00:20:25,905 --> 00:20:31,527
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the next paragraph says, the mind does not pull into grievous phenomena, disagreeable phenomena, not repulsive.

192
00:20:31,567 --> 00:20:34,127
[SPEAKER_00]: In this way bhikkhu is restrained.

193
00:20:34,347 --> 00:20:39,949
[SPEAKER_00]: A strong stake or post, this, bhikkhus, is a designation for mindfulness of the body.

194
00:20:39,989 --> 00:20:41,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, bhikkhus, you should train in this way.

195
00:20:41,970 --> 00:20:48,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the body will be developed by us, made much of, made a vehicle, made a ground, carried out, practiced, properly undertaken.

196
00:20:49,392 --> 00:20:51,953
[SPEAKER_00]: In this way, bhikkhus, you should train.

197
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:06,523
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then we have a... so you think of 43 and... but maybe... maybe there can be path 3 now, because the topic changes.

198
00:21:08,344 --> 00:21:13,146
[SPEAKER_00]: It talks about the undetermined and the path to undetermined, asankhata.

199
00:21:13,826 --> 00:21:16,307
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, so we can maybe do that later.

